1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,005
Lesson 2.3.

2
00:00:02,385 --> 00:00:04,219
The Fokker–Planck equation.

3
00:00:05,350 --> 00:00:08,273
A single Langevin
trajectory is unpredictable.

4
00:00:08,653 --> 00:00:11,492
But if we release thousands
of particles, their

5
00:00:11,492 --> 00:00:14,452
density evolves in a
perfectly deterministic way.

6
00:00:14,832 --> 00:00:18,416
The equation governing that density
is the Fokker–Planck equation.

7
00:00:18,796 --> 00:00:22,595
It is the bridge between the physics
of one noisy particle and the

8
00:00:22,595 --> 00:00:26,393
statistics of its distribution,
and it is the tool to prove that a

9
00:00:26,393 --> 00:00:29,789
thermodynamic chip ends up
sampling the right distribution.

10
00:00:30,917 --> 00:00:32,828
Think of a drop of ink in water.

11
00:00:33,208 --> 00:00:37,518
Each ink molecule does its
own Brownian motion, but the

12
00:00:37,518 --> 00:00:41,358
blot as a whole spreads
smoothly and predictably.

13
00:00:41,738 --> 00:00:46,325
If there is also a current in the
water, the blot drifts while it spreads.

14
00:00:46,705 --> 00:00:50,892
Fokker–Planck is exactly that:
a drift term, which moves the

15
00:00:50,892 --> 00:00:55,358
density along the force, and a
diffusion term, which spreads it.

16
00:00:56,483 --> 00:01:06,173
For the overdamped Langevin equation,
the density p(x, t) obeys ∂p/∂t = −∂J/∂x,

17
00:01:06,553 --> 00:01:12,190
where the probability current
is J = μ f p − D ∂p/∂x.

18
00:01:12,570 --> 00:01:17,676
The first term is drift: probability
travels with velocity μ f.

19
00:01:18,056 --> 00:01:20,863
The second is diffusion:
probability flows from

20
00:01:20,863 --> 00:01:23,431
where there is more to
where there is less.

21
00:01:23,811 --> 00:01:26,459
It is a continuity
equation: probability is

22
00:01:26,459 --> 00:01:29,229
neither created nor
destroyed, it only moves.

23
00:01:30,350 --> 00:01:31,160
Let's compare.

24
00:01:31,540 --> 00:01:35,320
The green bars are the histogram
of four thousand Langevin

25
00:01:35,320 --> 00:01:39,360
particles that all start at the
same point, in the right well.

26
00:01:39,740 --> 00:01:42,599
The amber curve is the
numerical solution of the

27
00:01:42,599 --> 00:01:45,279
Fokker–Planck equation,
with no noise at all.

28
00:01:45,659 --> 00:01:47,277
They match at every moment.

29
00:01:47,657 --> 00:01:52,608
First the density spreads within the well;
then, slowly, probability crosses the

30
00:01:52,608 --> 00:01:57,374
barrier and fills the other well until
it reaches the Boltzmann distribution.

31
00:01:58,750 --> 00:02:01,154
In the stationary state, the density does

32
00:02:01,154 --> 00:02:03,440
not change, so the current is constant.

33
00:02:03,820 --> 00:02:06,522
In one dimension, with
the density going to

34
00:02:06,522 --> 00:02:09,223
zero at the ends, the
current must be zero.

35
00:02:09,603 --> 00:02:15,974
Then drift exactly balances
diffusion: μ f p = D ∂p/∂x.

36
00:02:16,354 --> 00:02:24,341
With f = −V' and the Einstein relation,
the solution is p ∝ e^(−V/k_B T).

37
00:02:24,721 --> 00:02:28,475
Here is the promised proof:
Langevin dynamics with the right

38
00:02:28,475 --> 00:02:32,292
noise has the Boltzmann distribution
as its stationary state.

39
00:02:33,417 --> 00:02:36,072
But not every stationary
state is equilibrium.

40
00:02:36,452 --> 00:02:39,580
Put a particle on a ring with
a wavy potential and push

41
00:02:39,580 --> 00:02:42,367
it with a constant force
that makes it go around.

42
00:02:42,747 --> 00:02:46,263
The density stops changing,
but the current is not zero:

43
00:02:46,263 --> 00:02:49,215
there is a net flow of
probability circulating.

44
00:02:49,595 --> 00:02:52,102
It is a non-equilibrium steady state.

45
00:02:52,482 --> 00:02:55,057
The distribution is no
longer Boltzmann, and

46
00:02:55,057 --> 00:02:57,399
the system dissipates heat continuously.

47
00:02:57,779 --> 00:03:00,737
These states are the
protagonists of unit three.

48
00:03:01,850 --> 00:03:05,628
For a quadratic potential the
equation is solved exactly.

49
00:03:06,008 --> 00:03:10,705
If the particle starts at x₀, the
density is Gaussian at every instant,

50
00:03:11,085 --> 00:03:21,432
with mean x₀ e^(−t/τ) and variance (k_B
T/κ)(1 − e^(−2t/τ)), with τ = 1/(μκ).

51
00:03:21,812 --> 00:03:25,268
The mean relaxes with time τ and
the variance with half of it.

52
00:03:25,648 --> 00:03:31,632
With x₀ = 2, κ = 1 and
k_B T = 0.25, at t = τ

53
00:03:31,632 --> 00:03:36,819
the mean is 0.74 and the variance 0.22.

54
00:03:37,950 --> 00:03:42,286
Fokker–Planck gives us the guarantee
that a Langevin device samples the

55
00:03:42,286 --> 00:03:46,194
right distribution, and its
spectrum tells us how long it takes.

56
00:03:46,574 --> 00:03:50,502
The diffusion models of
unit nine are, at bottom,

57
00:03:50,502 --> 00:03:54,431
Fokker–Planck equations
run forward and backward.

58
00:03:54,811 --> 00:03:59,299
And in unit three the probability
current will give us entropy production.

59
00:04:00,417 --> 00:04:03,072
Fokker–Planck is the
continuous version of the

60
00:04:03,072 --> 00:04:05,669
evolution of distributions
in a Markov chain.

61
00:04:06,049 --> 00:04:10,303
Its exactly solvable case is
the Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process.

62
00:04:10,683 --> 00:04:14,084
Its analog for discrete
jumps is the master equation.

63
00:04:14,464 --> 00:04:17,259
And entropy production is
written with the current.

64
00:04:18,383 --> 00:04:21,881
Common mistakes: confusing
stationary with equilibrium.

65
00:04:22,261 --> 00:04:24,212
Forgetting the boundary conditions.

66
00:04:24,592 --> 00:04:27,112
Ignoring that with
state-dependent noise the

67
00:04:27,112 --> 00:04:29,345
equation depends on the interpretation.

68
00:04:29,725 --> 00:04:32,187
And thinking a single
trajectory follows the

69
00:04:32,187 --> 00:04:34,817
equation, when the equation
is for the density.

70
00:04:35,950 --> 00:04:41,164
In summary: the density of a
Langevin process obeys ∂p/∂t =

71
00:04:41,164 --> 00:04:45,847
−∂J/∂x, with a current
combining drift and diffusion.

72
00:04:46,227 --> 00:04:50,302
At equilibrium the current is zero
and the solution is Boltzmann.

73
00:04:50,682 --> 00:04:53,107
If the current does not vanish, we have a

74
00:04:53,107 --> 00:04:55,768
non-equilibrium steady
state that dissipates.

75
00:04:56,883 --> 00:05:01,669
To practice: write the equation for a
quadratic potential, prove that Boltzmann

76
00:05:01,669 --> 00:05:06,212
makes the current vanish, compute the
Gaussian solution at another instant,

77
00:05:06,592 --> 00:05:11,072
and explain why a constant force on
a ring cannot lead to equilibrium.
